        ":question/id": "918b2263-6a41-5532-8840-aa9f0d567ce6",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
        ":question/problem": "Find the arc length of the curve $r(t)=\\langle e^{2 t} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},e^{2 t} \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\rangle$ from $t=0$ to $t=1$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186119744/plots/curve-ac7f54a36c382e7b139cfe4a.png)",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we find $r'(t)$ and $\\|r'(t)\\|:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r'(t)&=\\left\\langle\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(e^{2 t} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}),\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(e^{2 t} \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\right\\rangle \\\\ &=\\left\\langle - e^{2 t} \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 e^{2 t} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},2 e^{2 t} \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + e^{2 t} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right\\rangle \\\\ \\|r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(- e^{2 t} \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 e^{2 t} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})^2+(2 e^{2 t} \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + e^{2 t} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})^2} \\\\ &=e^{2 t}\\sqrt{(2\\cos(t)-1\\sin(t))^2+(2\\sin(t)+1\\cos(t))^2} \\\\ &=e^{2 t}\\sqrt{4\\cos(t)^2-4\\cos(t)\\sin(t)+1\\sin(t)^2+4\\sin(t)^2+4\\cos(t)\\sin(t)+1\\cos(t)^2} \\\\ &=e^{2 t}\\sqrt{5(\\cos^2(t)+\\sin^2(t))} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{5} e^{2 t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{5} e^{2 t}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\sqrt{5} \\int_{0}^{1}e^{2 t}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\frac{\\sqrt{5} e^{2 t}}{2}\\big|_{0}^{1} \\\\ &=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}\\left(e^{2}-1\\right) \\\\ &=- \\frac{\\sqrt{5} \\left(1 - e^{2}\\right)}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
