        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
        ":question/problem": "Find the arc length of the curve $r(t)=\\langle e^{2 t} \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)},e^{2 t} \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\rangle$ from $t=1$ to $t=2$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186119744/plots/curve-c79de5b3410b597646e858d2.png)",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we find $r'(t)$ and $\\|r'(t)\\|:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r'(t)&=\\left\\langle\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(e^{2 t} \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}),\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(e^{2 t} \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)})\\right\\rangle \\\\ &=\\left\\langle - 3 e^{2 t} \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 2 e^{2 t} \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)},2 e^{2 t} \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 3 e^{2 t} \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right\\rangle \\\\ \\|r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(- 3 e^{2 t} \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 2 e^{2 t} \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)})^2+(2 e^{2 t} \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 3 e^{2 t} \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)})^2} \\\\ &=e^{2 t}\\sqrt{(2\\cos(3 t)-3\\sin(3 t))^2+(2\\sin(3 t)+3\\cos(3 t))^2} \\\\ &=e^{2 t}\\sqrt{4\\cos(3 t)^2-12\\cos(3 t)\\sin(3 t)+9\\sin(3 t)^2+4\\sin(3 t)^2+12\\cos(3 t)\\sin(3 t)+9\\cos(3 t)^2} \\\\ &=e^{2 t}\\sqrt{13(\\cos^2(3 t)+\\sin^2(3 t))} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{13} e^{2 t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L&=\\int_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{13} e^{2 t}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\sqrt{13} \\int_{1}^{2}e^{2 t}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\frac{\\sqrt{13} e^{2 t}}{2}\\big|_{1}^{2} \\\\ &=\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2}\\left(e^{4}-e^{2}\\right) \\\\ &=- \\frac{\\sqrt{13} \\left(1 - e^{2}\\right) e^{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
