        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
        ":question/problem": "Find the arc length of the curve $r(t)=\\langle 6 t + 4,- \\frac{\\left(3 t + 2\\right)^{3}}{3} - \\frac{1}{3 t + 2}\\rangle$ from $t=0$ to $t=1$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186119744/plots/curve-f6564f1f8e4e81d56ad0d537.png)",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we find $r'(t)$ and $\\|r'(t)\\|:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r'(t)&=\\left\\langle\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(6 t + 4),\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(- \\frac{\\left(3 t + 2\\right)^{3}}{3} - \\frac{1}{3 t + 2})\\right\\rangle \\\\ &=\\left\\langle 6,- 3 \\left(3 t + 2\\right)^{2} + \\frac{3}{\\left(3 t + 2\\right)^{2}}\\right\\rangle \\\\ \\|r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(6)^2+(- 3 \\left(3 t + 2\\right)^{2} + \\frac{3}{\\left(3 t + 2\\right)^{2}})^2} \\\\ &=3\\sqrt{4+\\left(\\frac{1}{(3 t + 2)^2}-(3 t + 2)^2\\right)^2} \\\\ &=3\\sqrt{4+\\frac{1}{(3 t + 2)^4}-2+(3 t + 2)^4} \\\\ &=3\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{(3 t + 2)^4}+2+(3 t + 2)^4} \\\\ &=3\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{1}{(3 t + 2)^2}+(3 t + 2)^2\\right)^2} \\\\ &=3 \\left(3 t + 2\\right)^{2} + \\frac{3}{\\left(3 t + 2\\right)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $3 t + 2>0$ throughout the given interval.\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=3 t + 2$, $\\mathrm{d}u=3\\mathrm{d}t$.\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L&=\\int_{0}^{1}3 \\left(3 t + 2\\right)^{2} + \\frac{3}{\\left(3 t + 2\\right)^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{2}^{5}u^{2} + \\frac{1}{u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ &=\\frac{u^{3}}{3} - \\frac{1}{u}\\big|_{2}^{5} \\\\ &=1\\left[\\left(-\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{5^3}{3}\\right)-\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{2^3}{3}\\right)\\right] \\\\ &=\\frac{393}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
